            <ArticleTitle>Distinct metabolic pathways trigger adipocyte fat accumulation induced by high-carbohydrate and high-fat diets.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1138-43</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.nut.2014.02.017</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0899-9007(14)00122-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Several metabolic pathways may be associated with obesity development and may be differentially modulated by dietary constituents. The aim of this study was to access the adipocyte metabolic pathways that lead to lipid accumulation in fat cells of mice fed a high-carbohydrate or high-fat diet.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Male Swiss mice ages 7 to 8 wk were fed a standard laboratory rodent diet-chow diet, a high-carbohydrate (HC) diet, or a high-fat (HF) diet for 8 wk.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Animals fed the HC diet exhibited a high lipogenesis rate and lipoprotein lipase activity even in a fasted state. Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma and sterol regulatory element-binding proteins-1 mRNAs were increased in adipose tissue. The HF diet did not promote the lipogenic pathways but attenuated lipolytic activity and glucose uptake in adipocytes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The HC diet induces constitutive expression of lipogenic transcription factors during fasting, whereas the HF diet decreases lipolysis in adipocytes. Both pathways may contribute to increase fat stores and maintain an obese state.</AbstractText>
